---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../utils/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How We Test and Evaluate"
  description="How Cozy Tiny Space checks weight limits, removal steps and measurements: manufacturer ratings, maker instructions, and honest failure notes — no copied numbers."
  canonicalPath="/how-we-test/"
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-4 pt-10">
    <p class="text-[0.75rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-accent">Our standard</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-[clamp(2rem,5vw,3rem)] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
      How we test and evaluate
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed text-muted">
      A storage tip is only useful if the shelf stays up and the deposit comes
      back. This page explains where every number on {SITE.name} comes from,
      and what we refuse to guess.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-8 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-[1.4rem] font-semibold text-ink">Weight limits come from the maker, not from other blogs</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Every adhesive hook, strip and tension product in our guides is rated
          by its manufacturer — 3M publishes a rating for every Command strip
          size, for example. We quote those published ratings, state them per
          strip or per hook (never per "set" unless the maker does), and then
          tell you to stay well under them, because heat, steam, dust and
          textured surfaces all reduce what a product actually holds.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-[1.4rem] font-semibold text-ink">Removal steps follow the manufacturer's instructions</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          "Damage-free" is a promise about move-out day, so removal matters as
          much as installation. Our removal guidance follows the maker's own
          instructions — for adhesive strips, that means holding the hook
          steady and pulling the tab straight down, slowly, never outward. If
          a product has no safe removal method for a surface, we say so and
          recommend a freestanding alternative instead.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-[1.4rem] font-semibold text-ink">Measurements are real and dual-unit</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Dimensions in our guides come from product specifications and
          standard furniture and appliance sizes, and are always given in both
          imperial and metric. If a fix needs a minimum surface size, a
          clearance or a curing time, the guide states it before the first
          step — not in a footnote after something has fallen down.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-[1.4rem] font-semibold text-ink">Failures are part of the guide</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Every method has surfaces and situations where it fails: suction on
          textured tile, adhesives near stove heat, tension rods on soft
          paint. Each guide carries a "what didn't work / who should skip
          this" note, because a renter who avoids one bad purchase saves more
          than a renter who finds one good one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-[1.4rem] font-semibold text-ink">Images follow a licence-first policy</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Photos on this site are our own, licensed from a stock library, or
          used with the creator's written permission and a visible credit.
          Credit alone is not permission, and we do not treat it as such.
          Decorative AI imagery is labelled and is never presented as a real
          install or a test result. See our
          <a href="/copyright/">Copyright &amp; Takedown</a> page if you are a
          creator with a question about an image.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-[1.4rem] font-semibold text-ink">Corrections</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Found a number that disagrees with a manufacturer's current spec, or
          a method that failed exactly as installed? Email
          <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> — corrections are
          checked against the source and the guide is updated with a new
          "last updated" date.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
